 ## Overview
-Face Culling is a mesh generation optimization for voxel-based volume rendering that reduces the number of vertices and triangles sent to the GPU by skipping the rendering of voxel faces that are adjacent to existing neighboring voxels [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of naively rendering all 6 faces of every cube, including those hidden by neighbors, which results in excessive draw calls and vertex processing overhead. Unlike [Block Visibility Culling](5334-block-visibility-culling.md) which operates on entire blocks or [Opacity-based Mesh Culling](8759-opacity-based-mesh-culling.md) which considers material properties, Face Culling operates strictly on geometric adjacency within the voxel grid to eliminate internal faces [S1, unofficial].
+Face Culling is a mesh generation optimization for blocky voxel meshes that reduces the number of vertices sent to the GPU by skipping the rendering of voxel faces that are adjacent to existing neighboring voxels [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of naively rendering all 6 faces of every cube, including those hidden by neighbors [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings by performing a direct per-voxel neighbor check during mesh assembly to determine face visibility, rather than using coverage masks, backface culling, or opacity-based thresholds [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method iterates through occupied voxel positions and examines their six adjacent neighbors. For each face of a voxel, the algorithm determines visibility based on the occupancy of the neighbor in the direction of the face's normal vector $\mathbf{n}$.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A set of occupied voxel positions in a 3D grid, typically represented as a hash set or grid lookup structure.
+*   **Output:** A vertex buffer containing only the exposed faces of the voxels.
 
